<h1>Year-End Backup Checksum Audit: A Practical Guide</h1>
<p>The end of the year is a good time to check whether important files are backed up and still readable. A backup is not truly useful until you know it can be restored. Checksums help with one part of that process: confirming that copied files match their original versions or that archived files have not changed since the checksum was recorded.</p>
<p>A year-end checksum audit does not need to be complex. Choose the important files first: accounting exports, legal documents, client archives, source-code releases, family photos, scanned IDs, and database dumps. For each archive or important file, calculate a SHA-256 checksum and save it in a text manifest with the file name and date.</p>
<p>SHA-256 is a good default because it is widely supported and defined in NIST's <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final">Secure Hash Standard</a>. MD5 may still exist in old backup systems, but for new manifests, SHA-256 is the better choice. If an older tool creates only MD5 values, document that limitation and consider updating the workflow.</p>
<p>After copying files to external storage or cloud storage, calculate the hash again from the destination file. If the source and destination hashes match, the copied file is identical at the time you checked it. If they do not match, do not delete the original. Recopy, check disk health, and confirm that you are comparing the same file version.</p>
<p>A checksum audit also helps detect silent changes. If an archive's hash changes but no one intentionally updated the file, investigate. The cause may be a new export, a repaired archive, metadata changes, line-ending changes, or corruption. The checksum will not explain the reason, but it tells you where to look.</p>
<p>Use consistent naming. A manifest such as checksums-2027-12-02.txt is easier to understand later than a file called hashes.txt. Include the algorithm name beside each value. A future reader should not need to guess whether a 64-character value is SHA-256 or something else.</p>
<p>Tools are available on every major system. Windows users can use certutil or PowerShell Get-FileHash. Linux users can use sha256sum. macOS users can use openssl dgst. The commands are covered in <a href="/verify-file-checksums-windows-mac-linux.html">How to Verify File Checksums on Windows, macOS, and Linux</a>.</p>
<p>Keep at least one copy of the manifest outside the storage location being checked. If a drive fails, a checksum file stored only on that drive may disappear with it. A printed report, password manager note, repository file, or separate cloud folder can preserve the reference values you need for later verification.</p>
<p>For large folders, hash archives rather than every loose file when that matches the recovery goal. If you need file-level recovery, keep file-level manifests. If you need to confirm that a compressed yearly archive stayed intact, a single SHA-256 value for the archive may be enough and easier to manage.</p>
<p>The final step is restoration testing. A checksum proves matching bytes, not that your backup process is complete. Open a few files, restore a sample archive, and make sure you can access the storage account or external drive. Checksums are one useful control in a backup routine, not the whole routine.</p>
